=== QF001 | a builtin used as a parameter
f:{[count] count+1}

=== QF001 | a q keyword from .Q.res, not just the .q namespace
f:{[sin] sin*2}

=== QF001 | qSQL keywords are reserved too
f:{[from] from}

=== clean | a name that merely resembles a builtin is fine
f:{[cnt] cnt+1}

=== clean | `find` and `fill` are operators in q, not names it reserves
f:{[find] find}

=== clean | a builtin name inside a longer name is not the builtin
f:{[counted] counted+1}

=== clean | a builtin as a namespaced field is not a local shadow
.email.subject:"hi"

=== QF002 | the drop operator cannot be a parameter
f:{[_] x+1}

=== QF004 | a definition inside a namespace that shadows a builtin
\d .util
count:{[t] 42}
\d .

=== clean | a file may end inside a namespace it opened
\d .util
helper:{[x] x}

=== clean | a later directive back to the root closes the file
\d .util
helper:{[x] x}
\d .

=== clean | the same name written dotted is not inside the namespace block
.util.count:{[t] 42}

=== QF005 | a nested lambda cannot see the enclosing local
f:{[a] g:{[b] a+b}; g[1]}

=== clean | passing the outer local in explicitly is the fix
f:{[a] g:{[a;b] a+b}; g[a;1]}

=== QF007 | a call in the parameter slot: q refuses the source outright
f:{[tables[]] x+1}

=== QF007 | an expression in the parameter slot
f:{[a+b] 1}

=== QF007 | an integer literal reads as body text, not a parameter
f:{[1] x+1}

=== QF007 | a symbol literal likewise
f:{[`s] x+1}

=== QF007 | two names without a separator is one bracket expression
f:{[a b] a}

=== QF007 | an index in the parameter slot
f:{[a[0]] a}

=== QF007 | an assignment in the parameter slot
f:{[a:1] a}

=== QF007 | a string literal, which the rules see only as blanks
f:{["s"] 1}

=== clean | a dotted name is a legal parameter, surprising as that is
f:{[.q.z] 1}

=== clean | underscores inside a name are fine
f:{[a_b] a_b+1}

=== clean | whitespace around the separators
f:{[ a ; b ] a+b}

=== QF010 | no space before the body does not hide the global x
f:{[a]x+1}

=== clean | the same shape where the body uses its parameter
f:{[a]a+1}

=== clean | the empty parameter list
f:{[] 42}

=== clean | implicit arguments, with no signature to contradict
f:{x+y}

=== QF008 | a parameter declared twice
f:{[a;a] a}

=== QF008 QF016 | and not only adjacently, and `b` is left unread
f:{[a;b;a] a}

=== QF009 QF016 | a trailing separator is a third parameter, named `2` by q
f:{[a;b;] a}

=== QF009 | a leading one likewise
f:{[;a] a}

=== QF010 | x is a global here, not an argument: q throws 'x
f:{[a] x+1}

=== QF010 | the same for z
f:{[a] z*2}

=== clean | a global x that does exist is a legitimate reference
x:1;f:{[a] x+1}

=== clean | x declared as the parameter is the argument
f:{[x] x+1}

=== clean | a column named x in qSQL is not the implicit argument
f:{[t] select x from t}

=== QF001 | a join keyword is reserved even though it reads like a name
f:{[aj] aj}

=== QF001 | control words are reserved
f:{[while] while}

=== QF001 | several at once
f:{[count;til] count til}

=== clean | a name q does not reserve, however builtin it sounds
f:{[median] median}

=== QF012 | a reserved name assigned at the root
count:42

=== QF012 | one q silently accepts, and the linter still flags
from:1

=== QF003 QF017 | a local shadow at column zero is not root scope, and nothing reads it
f:{[x]
count:1
}

=== QF013 | a builtin column name in a table literal
t:([] first:1 2 3)

=== QF013 | the second column is checked too
t:([] px:1 2; last:3 4)

=== clean | ordinary column names
t:([] px:1 2; qty:3 4)

=== QF014 | read before the assignment that makes it local
f:{[x] r:cfg; cfg:1; r}

=== QF014 | a read in a condition, assigned in the branch
f:{[x] if[a;a:1]}

=== QF014 | amend is a read of the unset local
f:{a+:1;a:2}

=== clean | assigned first, read after
f:{[x] cfg:1; r:cfg; r}

=== clean | a parameter is set from the start
f:{[a] b:a; b:b+1; b}

=== clean | a global assignment does not make the name local
f:{[x] r:cfg; cfg::1; r}

=== clean | column names in a query resolve to the table
f:{[t] r:select from t where px>1; px:2; r}

=== QF015 | return is not a q keyword
f:{[x] return x+1}

=== QF015 | else is not either
if[a;1] else 2

=== QF015 | true and false are undefined names
f:{[x] $[x;true;false]}

=== clean | null is a q function
f:{[x] null x}

=== clean | a file that defines the name has made it one
return:{[x] x}
f:{[x] return x}

=== clean | a symbol is not a name
s:`return

=== clean | within one statement q evaluates right to left, so this assigns first
f:{[x] x#a where (a:x mod 7) in 2 3}

=== QF017 | a table-literal column is not an assignment, but the trailing local is unread
f:{[x] if[x; :([] range_from:`timestamp$())]; range_from:1}

=== clean | a parameter named like a keyword is a name
f:{[func;return] if[()~return; func]}

=== clean | call arguments run right to left, so the later slot assigns first
f:{[x] g[c-1; c:count x]}

=== clean | two expressions side by side: the right one runs first
f:{[y] $[1=n; first; flip] $[1=n:depth y; enlist; flip] y}

=== clean | a control bracket nested in a call slot still runs left to right within itself
f:{[x] g[$[c;1;2]; c:1]}

=== QF014 | but a control bracket orders its own slots
f:{[x] g[if[c; c:1]]}

=== clean | multiple assignment binds its names
f:{[x;y] (tx;ty):type each (x;y); if[tx=11h; tx:0h]; tx+ty}

=== QF005 | the names a multiple assignment binds are locals, which a nested lambda cannot see
f:{[x] (a;b):x; g:{[v] v+a}; g 1}

=== QF016 | a parameter the body never reads
f:{[used;stale] used+1}

=== clean | a lambda that reads none of them is conforming to a signature
f:{[t] 42}

=== clean | every parameter read
f:{[a;b] a+b}

=== clean | a name that says it is unused is the author saying so
f:{[a;unusedFlag] a+1}

=== clean | a symbol is not a use of the name it spells
f:{[a] `a`b}

=== QF017 | a local assigned and never read
f:{[a] b:1; a}

=== clean | read later in the body
f:{[a] b:1; a+b}

=== clean | read inside a bracket, which is where locals usually are read
f:{[a] b:1; $[a>0; b; 0]}

=== clean | a qSQL column alias is not a local
f:{[t] select mid:(bid+ask)%2 from t}

=== clean | a table-literal column is not one either
f:{[] ([sym:`symbol$()] px:`float$())}

=== clean | a name saying it is unused is the author saying so
f:{[a] unusedTmp:1; a}

=== clean | rebinding a parameter is ordinary q, not a finding
f:{[x] x:x where x>0; x}

=== QF018 | assigning an unknown name does not define the source
f:{[] aa:bb; aa}

=== QF018 | a standalone unknown read is suspicious too
f:{[] bb; 1}

=== QF018 | returning an unknown name is a read
f:{[] bb}

=== clean profile: general | unresolved globals are uncertain, not proven runtime errors
f:{[] aa:bb; aa}

=== clean | a parameter supplies the assignment value
f:{[bb] aa:bb; aa}

=== clean | an implicit parameter supplies the assignment value
f:{aa:x; aa}

=== clean | an earlier local supplies the assignment value
f:{[] bb:1; aa:bb; aa}

=== QF014 | an unset local already has a definite diagnostic
f:{[] aa:bb; bb:1; aa}

=== clean | a namespace global remains available inside a function
\d .example
bb:1
f:{[] aa:bb; aa}
\d .

=== clean | a fully qualified definition supplies a namespace global
.example.bb:1
\d .example
f:{[] aa:bb; aa}
\d .

=== clean | a global defined later is available when the function runs
\d .example
f:{[] aa:bb; aa}
bb:1
\d .

=== QF018 | the same name in another namespace is not a definition here
.other.bb:1
\d .example
f:{[] aa:bb; aa}
\d .

=== clean | a global write inside another function supplies the name
init:{[] bb::1}
f:{[] aa:bb; aa}

=== clean | literal set defines the global
`bb set 1
f:{[] aa:bb; aa}

=== clean | a global multiple assignment defines both names
(bb;cc):1 2
f:{[] aa:bb; aa}

=== clean | a builtin is a valid assignment value
f:{[] aa:sum; aa}

=== clean | a symbol is a literal, not a variable read
f:{[] aa:`bb; aa}

=== clean | masked strings must not turn an application into a bare read
f:{[] aa:"J"$bb; aa}

=== clean | qualified references may be supplied by another module
f:{[] aa:.external.bb; aa}

=== clean | a load can supply globals from another file
\l globals.q
f:{[] aa:bb; aa}

=== clean | dynamic evaluation can supply globals
value "bb:1"
f:{[] aa:bb; aa}

=== clean | qSQL column aliases are not assignment values in local scope
f:{[t] select aa:bb from t}

=== QF018 | a query elsewhere in the function does not hide a simple read
f:{[t] aa:bb; aa; select from t}

=== QF005 | an enclosing local already has a nested-scope diagnostic
f:{[bb] g:{[] aa:bb; aa}; g[]}

=== QF010 | an undeclared implicit parameter already has a diagnostic
f:{[] aa:x; aa}

=== QF018 | quoted text in comments does not hide a real read
f:{[]
    / a "value" copied from somewhere
    aa:bb;
    aa}

=== clean | string application is not a simple value read
f:{[] "key" bb}

=== clean | applying a nested lambda is not a simple value read
f:{[] {[v] v} bb}

=== clean | a namespace multiple assignment defines both names
\d .example
(bb;cc):1 2
f:{[] aa:bb; aa}
\d .

=== QF018 | an unrelated root global does not define a namespace global
bb:1
\d .example
f:{[] aa:bb; aa}
\d .

=== QF018 | a namespace definition does not supply a misspelled root name
\d .dasd
bbb:2;
\d .
bb;

=== QF018 | the same spelling in another namespace does not supply a root name
\d .dasd
bb:2;
\d .
bb;

=== QF018 | a top-level assignment still reads its source
aa:bb;
aa;

=== QF018 | a bare root read needs no trailing semicolon
bb

=== QF018 | a top-level x is not an implicit parameter
x;

=== clean | a root definition supplies a top-level read
bb:2;
bb;

=== clean | a namespace definition supplies a read in that namespace
\d .dasd
bb:2;
bb;
\d .

=== clean | qualified references remain outside this check
\d .dasd
bbb:2;
\d .
.dasd.bbb;

=== clean | a multiline expression is not a bare name read
show
    bb

=== clean | a multiline bracketed expression is not a bare name read
r:(
bb;
cc)

=== clean | a multiline lambda is checked once in its own scope
f:{[bb]
    aa:bb;
    aa}

=== QF018 | a top-level read after a multiline lambda is checked separately
f:{[bb]
    bb}
bb;

=== clean | text in comments and strings is not a root read
/ bb;
"bb;"

=== clean | system commands and their arguments are not q name reads
\v
\c 25 80

=== clean | loaded files may supply a root global
\l globals.q
bb;

=== clean profile: general | root unresolved names remain uncertain warnings
bb;
